{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "AI Code Review & QA Agent",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/acme/ai-code-review",
          "version": "deepseek-v4-flash",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "acr/security",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded payments API key committed to the repository"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agent"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "acr/bug",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "applyDiscount treats a percentage as a fraction, producing negative totals"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "bug",
                  "agent"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "acr/maintainability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PAYMENTS_API_KEY is dead code"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "agent"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "acr/testing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "orderQuery and saveOrder are added without any test coverage"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "testing",
                  "agent"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/security",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hardcoded payments API key committed to the repository\n\nA live-looking payments credential is embedded in source: `const PAYMENTS_API_KEY = ...` with the comment \"Staging key for the payments sandbox — rotate it before going live.\" Committing a secret to git means it is permanently in history and readable by anyone with repo access; the comment's promise to rotate later does not undo the leak. Remove the literal, read the key from an environment variable (e.g. `process.env.PAYMENTS_API_KEY`) or a secret manager, and rotate the exposed key immediately since it is already in the branch history."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/security",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "orderQuery builds SQL by string concatenation (SQL injection)\n\n`orderQuery` interpolates `tenantId` directly into the SQL text: `\"SELECT id, total FROM orders WHERE tenant = '\" + tenantId + \"'\"`. Any caller-supplied tenant id such as `x' OR '1'='1` turns this into a full-table read, and `'; DROP TABLE orders; --` into data destruction. Use a parameterised query (pass the value as a bind parameter) or at minimum validate/escape the identifier before concatenation."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 11
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "confidence": 0.9,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/bug",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "applyDiscount treats a percentage as a fraction, producing negative totals\n\n`total - total * discountPercent` multiplies by the raw percent value, so a 10% discount is applied as 10x the total. `applyDiscount(100, 10)` returns -900, i.e. the customer is charged a negative amount. The new test at test/pricing.test.js:8 asserts exactly this wrong value, so the suite passes while the behaviour is broken. Divide by 100 (`total - total * (discountPercent / 100)`) and update the test to expect 90."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 7
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "confidence": 0.9,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/bug",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "saveOrder reports success without awaiting or checking the insert\n\n`saveOrder` is declared `async` but calls `store.insert(...)` without `await` and unconditionally returns `{ ok: true }`. If `insert` is asynchronous (or throws/rejects), the caller receives `ok: true` before the write completes and any rejection becomes an unhandled rejection rather than a reported failure — silent data loss. Fix: `await store.insert(...)` and let errors propagate (or catch and return `{ ok: false, error }`)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 15
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "confidence": 0.8,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/bug",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "saveOrder is async but never awaits the store insert and always reports success\n\n`saveOrder` is declared `async` yet calls `store.insert(...)` without `await` and unconditionally returns `{ ok: true }`. If the store's insert is asynchronous (the usual case for a DB-backed store), a rejected insert becomes an unhandled rejection while the caller still receives `{ ok: true }`, so a failed order write is silently reported as saved. Await the insert and let the error propagate, or return the insert result."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 15
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "confidence": 0.55,
            "status": "suppressed",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/maintainability",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "PAYMENTS_API_KEY is dead code\n\nThe constant is declared but never read anywhere in the repository (search for `PAYMENTS_API_KEY` matches only its declaration). Beyond the secret-leak issue, it is unused code that will drift. Fix: remove it along with the credential."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "low",
            "confidence": 0.6,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/bug",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "isPaid uses loose equality, so non-string values coerce to 'paid'\n\n`status == 'paid'` performs type coercion, so values such as an object whose `toString()` returns 'paid', or `['paid']`, compare equal and are treated as paid orders. Use strict equality (`status === 'paid'`) so only the exact string is accepted."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 20
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "low",
            "confidence": 0.5,
            "status": "suppressed",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/bug",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "isPaid uses loose equality, so non-string values can pass\n\n`return status == 'paid';` uses `==`, so values that coerce to the string 'paid' (e.g. an object with a matching `toString`, or `['paid']`) are treated as paid. For a payment-status check this is a correctness/authorisation-adjacent risk. Fix: use `status === 'paid'`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pricing.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 20
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "low",
            "confidence": 0.6,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/testing",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "orderQuery and saveOrder are added without any test coverage\n\nThe new module exports `orderQuery` and `saveOrder`, but the test file imports only `applyDiscount` and `isPaid` (search for `orderQuery|saveOrder` finds no test reference). The two riskiest functions in the change — the SQL builder and the write path — are untested. Fix: add tests covering tenantId escaping/parameterisation and the insert-failure path of `saveOrder`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "test/pricing.test.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "low",
            "confidence": 0.85,
            "status": "validated",
            "source": "agent",
            "reviewRunId": "9af15ba5-ac8b-4301-a820-ac6010dd15ec"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "acr/testing",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Test pins the buggy discount result as expected behaviour\n\n`assert.equal(applyDiscount(100, 10), -900)` with the comment \"Pins today's behaviour: a 10 is treated as ten times the total.\" locks in the defect described above: the test will fail the moment the percentage bug is fixed, and it gives false confidence that the pricing helper is correct. Assert the intended contract (a 10% discount on 100 yields 90) and add a boundary case for 0 and 100 percent."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "test/pricing.test.js"
